  "title": "Inside the Mighty Engine",
  "hook": "Brian drew a super engine on his cardboard car and shouted VROOM — so why won't it move?",
  "reading_minutes": 7,
  "core_lesson": "Noise doesn't move cars — energy does. When you're stuck, ask someone who knows.",
  "new_terms": [
    "energy",
    "engine"
  ],
  "adult_safety_note": "No real engines, fuel or batteries are handled. Auntie Ada uses picture cards and the toy Bolt. Children build a paper sail with tape and a paper straw and move it with their own breath.",
  "localisation_key_prefix": "s01e04",
  "editorial_status": "draft",
  "learning_objectives": [
    {
      "topic": "Science",
      "goal": "Things need energy to move; noise is not what moves a car."
    },
    {
      "topic": "System",
      "goal": "Bolt's simplified energy path: battery → switch and wires → motor → axle → wheels."
    },
    {
      "topic": "Sequencing",
      "goal": "Put the steps of an energy path in order, from where energy starts to where it ends."
    },
    {
      "topic": "Real vehicles",
      "goal": "Some cars burn fuel in an engine; electric cars use a big battery and motor. Real cars have many more parts."
    },
    {
      "topic": "Asking for expertise",
      "goal": "Brian stops guessing and asks Auntie Ada, who knows real engines."
    },
    {
      "topic": "Safety",
      "goal": "Real engines get hot and have moving parts — only look with a grown-up; children use pictures and paper models."
    }
  ],
  "talk_together": [
    "Why didn't shouting VROOM make Brian's car move?",
    "Where did the energy for the sail car come from? Follow it back to the start.",
    "Who could you ask when you want to know how something works?"
